id: pm-gh1485
title: "GH-1485: Bound history-index reader contention before authoritative appends"
description: "Evaluate and bound writer delay from exclusive history-event-index readers while preserving authoritative history, invalidation, fallback and late-fact visibility. The reported mixed reader/writer workload retained correctness but breached its 2000ms real-time delivery SLO in one traced run."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 2
tags[4]: "area:history","area:locking",github-intake,performance
created_at: "2026-10-16T13:01:36.615Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-16T13:01:36.615Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "Reproduce with isolated traced and untraced cohorts and report writer lock wait separately from downstream detection; Choose and document a bounded snapshot/fair-lock/critical-section protocol from measured attribution; Preserve authoritative fallback, committed invalidation and late-fact visibility; Prove zero lost or duplicate events, zero history drift and the declared end-to-end latency budget under mixed SDK and CLI writers."
goal: project management = context management
objective: Measure reader-versus-writer lock attribution and implement a bounded history-index read/write protocol without losing committed facts.
value: UI and agent watchers can observe project context without delaying authoritative mutations beyond a measured collaboration budget.
why_now: GH-1485 reports a 2247.5ms pre-append delay with at least 2071.0ms waiting for the history-index lock under 100 readers and ten writer lanes.
parent: pm-lvyd
risk: high
confidence: medium
severity: medium
expected_result: "History-index reads preserve committed-fact correctness while writer delay and end-to-end delivery remain within an explicit, reproducible collaboration budget."
actual_result: "Intake only: GH-1485 reports one breached traced run among three, 50 updates and 5000 receipts delivered exactly once, on a shared four-CPU host. The attribution includes scheduling/open/retry overhead and has not been reproduced here. No fix or performance acceptance is claimed."
component: history event index concurrency
